## Data Analysis

### Pareto-Front Candidates (both objectives measured, good quality)

Sorting by elastic modulus and pore fraction to identify non-dominated points:

| Condition | Modulus (bar) | Pore Fraction | Notes |
|-----------|--------------|---------------|-------|
| 17p5-0add-5deg-550s-N2-1450s | 912.84 | 0.27 | High modulus, low PF; top-edge gap |
| 19-0add-5deg-0s-N2-1500s (run1) | 767.83 | 0.34 | Good balance; clean membrane |
| 20-0add-5deg-150s-N2-1650s (run1) | 755.94 | 0.34 | Good balance; clean membrane |
| 17p5-0add-19deg-90s-N2-1800s (run1) | 860.49 | 0.27 | High modulus, low PF |
| 18-0add-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s (run3) | 385.22 | 0.41 | High PF, low modulus |
| 17p5-0add-15deg-400s-NoN2-1800s | 400.47 | 0.40 | High PF, low modulus |
| 17p5-0add-19deg-90s-run4 (19-run4) | 330.99 | 0.44 | Highest PF observed; very low modulus |

The Pareto frontier spans roughly:
- **High-modulus end**: ~860–913 bar at PF ~0.27
- **Balanced region**: ~750–770 bar at PF ~0.34 (runs of 19wt%/5°C and 20wt%/5°C)
- **High-PF end**: ~330–400 bar at PF ~0.40–0.44

### Validation Status Check

- **19-0add-5deg-0s-N2-1500s**: run1 (767 bar, PF=0.34, clean), run2 (no modulus), run3 (614 bar, PF=0.32, wrinkled), run4 (890 bar, PF=0.29, wrinkled). Runs 2–4 have quality issues or missing data; run1 remains the best clean measurement but not independently confirmed with both objectives clean.
- **20-0add-5deg-150s-N2-1650s**: run1 (756 bar, PF=0.34, clean), run2 (673 bar, PF=0.28, wrinkled), run3 (no modulus). Not confirmed.
- **17p5-0add-18deg-75s-N2-1800s**: run1 (937 bar, PF=0.27, single specimen), run2 (734 bar, PF=0.30), run6 (700 bar, PF=0.26, clean). High modulus but low PF; not Pareto-improving over balanced candidates.

### Key Observation: Balanced Pareto Region

The most attractive **balanced** Pareto candidates are around **polymer_wt ~19–20, bath_temp 5°C, low wait time, N2, moderate pullcast speed**. The 19wt%/5°C/0s/N2/1500s condition showed 767 bar / 0.34 PF in run1 (clean membrane), but subsequent runs had quality issues. The 20wt%/5°C/150s/N2/1650s run1 also showed 756 bar / 0.34 PF (clean).

### Gap in Coverage

Looking at the diversity context and Pareto analysis:
- **polymer_wt ~19–20** at **bath_temp 5°C** with **moderate pullcast speed (~5–8 mm/s)** and **short wait times** appears promising for the balanced region
- The 19wt% condition at 5°C with pullcast_speed=3 mm/s (very slow) produced variable results; pullcast_speed=5 mm/s with 30s wait (run: 19-0add-5deg-30s-N2-1500s) gave 745 bar / 0.27 PF with a wrinkle
- **19.5 wt% at 5°C bath** was only tested at high pullcast speed (18 mm/s) with poor quality

A promising unexplored region: **polymer_wt ~19.5, bath_temp 5°C, pullcast_speed ~6–8 mm/s, moderate wait ~60–90s, N2, nips_bath_wait 1500–1600s**. Higher polymer concentration tends to increase modulus; cold bath promotes phase separation for higher PF; moderate speed and short wait reduce wrinkling.

### Recommendation

I'll target **polymer_wt = 19.5, bath_temp = 5°C, pullcast_speed = 7 mm/s, coupon_to_bath_wait_time = 60s, N2 = true, nips_bath_wait_time = 1600s, mixing_temp = 60°C**. This is distinct from the 19.5wt%/5°C/18mm/s/0s run (which had coverage gaps and wrinkles at very high speed) and the 19.5wt%/25°C runs. The slower pullcast speed and short wait should reduce wrinkling while the cold bath and higher polymer concentration target the balanced Pareto region (high modulus + moderate-to-high pore fraction). This fills an under-sampled region and could reveal whether 19.5 wt% at 5°C with gentler casting conditions improves on the 19 wt% baseline.

